More specifically, Ashikaga Takauji was a renegade daimyo who led a 13 
year cival war in Japan against the shogun at that time.  He was a 
decendant of the Minamoto clan.  On July 8, 1336 his samurai entered 
Kyoto and installed a puppet prince on the 'throne'.  The previous 
ruler (Go-Daigo) escaped and formed the 'Southern Court' in a town 
called Yoshino.  The period between 1337 and 1392, when Japan was ruled 
by two courts, is known as the Nambokucho.

Western historians view Ashikaga Takauji as an greedy and manipulative 
man, who sought to bring down the legitimate rulers of his time.  
Others view him as the samurai's strongest defender of the time, who 
sought to overthrow a ruler who was attempting to dilute the authority 
of the samurai.



